course: dictionary
lesson: 12662
title: Words that fade away
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Dissipated comes from the verb dissipate. Its main meaning is to spread out until it disappears, or to waste something little by little. Think of morning fog: it does not vanish in one second. It slowly spreads out and thins until it is gone. That is dissipated.
explain: «The fog dissipated as the sun came up.» The sun warms the air, so the fog spreads out and goes. Dissipated means it disappeared slowly. Test it: swap in "disappeared slowly" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The word can also mean wasted. «Much of the engine's energy was dissipated as heat.» Some of the energy did not move the car. It spread out into the air as heat and was lost. In science, that is how energy is dissipated.
explain: Context gives you the clue. Ask: does the thing spread out and vanish, or is it used up for nothing? Then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it fits.
fact: Dissipate is a verb: dissipate, dissipates, dissipated, dissipating.
fact: «Dissipated» means spread out and slowly disappeared, like fog, smoke or heat.
fact: «Dissipated» can also mean wasted, like energy or money.
fact: In stories, anger or fear can dissipate, meaning it fades away gradually.
fact: An old, formal use describes a dissipated person: someone who has spent their life on drinking and wild living.
exercise: «The fog dissipated before lunch.» What does dissipated mean here? | Was painted bright red | *Spread out and slowly disappeared | Became thicker and darker | Froze solid
exercise: «Much of the engine's energy was dissipated as heat.» What does this tell us? | The engine grew bigger | *Energy was spread out and wasted | Some energy was spread out and wasted | Energy was stored in a battery forever
exercise: «Her anger dissipated after a long talk.» What does dissipated mean? | Turned into ice | Grew louder and stronger | Was paid back in full | *Faded away gradually
test: «The smoke dissipated into the sky.» Dissipated means? | Grew heavier | Became a solid lump | *Spread out until it was gone | Was carried away by a dog
test: «He dissipated all his savings on useless gadgets.» What does dissipated mean here? | Doubled in size | Saved carefully | *Wasted | Gave away to charity
exam: Which meaning fits best in «The crowd dissipated after the concert»? | *Spread out and drifted away | Gathered together in one place | Sat down for a nap | Got louder and rowdier
